  1. [1mThe expensive parts of multi-user aren't the notifications.[0m They're accounts, auth, permissions ("can you even see
     this task?"), and a server. If we pick multi-user, we're signing up to build all of that before a single
     notification fires — and "notify on change" is maybe 15% of that work.
  2. [1mThe seam is cheap to preserve, the backend is not.[0m If we build tasks so every mutation emits a change event
     ([38;5;153mtask.created[39m, [38;5;153mtask.updated[39m, [38;5;153mtask.completed[39m, with a timestamp and an actor field), then notifications are a
     consumer of that stream. Today the only actor is "you" and the only consumer is the UI. Later, a server can feed
     that same stream from other people's edits and the notification layer barely changes. Deciding to go multi-user
     later costs you the backend — which you'd pay anyway — not a notifications rewrite.

  [1mSo: start single-user, browser-only, with an explicit task-change event stream.[0m You get something working, and the one
  design decision that would be painful to retrofit is made correctly from day one.

  The other half of my recommendation is a reordering: [1mtasks come before notifications.[0m There's nothing to be notified
  [3mabout[0m yet. I'd rather design "tasks with a change-event seam" as the first project, then notifications as the second,
  than design a notification system against a task model we're inventing in our heads as we go.

  That said, "tasks they care about" is a real hint that you're picturing more than one person. If shared tasks are
  actually the point of the app — not a someday-maybe — say so, and the whole calculus changes.
